WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.208 --> 00:00:07.033
No, OK, stop. She's using
nothing to pick those.

00:00:07.033 --> 00:00:08.460
She's not using a lockpick.

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:10.590
She's just pulling at them,
and then they come off.

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:12.120
Hey, guys, I'm Matt Johnson,

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:16.140
and I'm an escape artist
for about 30 years now.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:17.518
Today, you and I are
going to be looking at

00:00:17.518 --> 00:00:19.050
movie and TV escapes,

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:22.980
and we're going to be judging
how real they actually are.

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:24.995
Back in Phillips' car, why?

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:29.820
They think we're idiots.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.573
Picking-a-set-of-handcuffs escape

00:00:31.573 --> 00:00:34.530
is used all the time by escape artists.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:38.640
These are standard police
regulation handcuffs here,

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.170
probably double-locking.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:43.320
So when you put them on, if
you don't double-lock it,

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.930
the ratchet can still push in
and could cause a lot of pain.

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:49.230
That to me looks like a blade
from a hacksaw or something.

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:51.870
You could use that to
get out of handcuffs.

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:54.150
There are three ways to get out
of handcuffs that I know of.

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:55.680
One is with a key.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.050
You could actually get
out of a pair of handcuffs

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:01.290
by fashioning a key with
something like a paper clip.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:04.950
And then a third way is
what's called a shim,

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:09.000
which is a flat piece of
steel, which he's using here.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.550
He's putting it between
the cuff and the ratchet

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.260
and sliding it in there.

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.780
And essentially, where this goes in, here,

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.760
you can see that little
space where it goes in.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:18.593
Well, if I lock that,

00:01:18.593 --> 00:01:21.450
those teeth are hid in a little lever here

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:25.080
that's got teeth on it,
which is sprung, right?

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:27.373
So when you push that
in, that little lever

00:01:27.373 --> 00:01:29.250
goes click, click,
click, click, like that.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.710
But because the teeth
are serrated one way,

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:33.420
now I can't pull it back out.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.870
So if I put a flat piece
of metal in that hole

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:41.040
and push it in and then just
push the cuff in one time,

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.557
it'll then slide out because
the flat piece of metal

00:01:43.557 --> 00:01:46.632
is between the serrated
teeth and the little ratchet.

00:01:51.713 --> 00:01:53.550
He's in the back of a police car.

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:55.680
He's stressing out probably,
but what you have to do

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:57.990
as an escape artist, you have
to lower your heart rate,

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:01.560
calm your nerves, and just
focus on the job at hand.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:03.090
I mean, he looks pretty calm.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:05.610
See, it's taken him 10
seconds or so to get out.

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:08.670
I mean, you can get out of
handcuffs a lot faster than that.

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:12.920
The record is a guy from Italy

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:14.370
called Alexis Arts,

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:16.950
in 0.9 seconds.

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.780
However, when you're put in things like,

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:20.262
they're behind your back,

00:02:20.262 --> 00:02:22.011
so you've got to find where you're at.

00:02:22.011 --> 00:02:25.003
Plus he's jammed up against
a seat and seat-belted in.

00:02:25.003 --> 00:02:27.030
He hasn't got a lot of room, right?

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:29.490
So I would actually say 10
seconds in that situation,

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.500
under duress, is actually pretty fast.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.470
I would actually give
this a high 9 or a 10.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:35.940
Let's try and get these ropes loose.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:38.235
We've got to get to Marcus
before the Nazis do.

00:02:38.235 --> 00:02:40.620
If Indie moved his left
arm even a little bit more,

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:42.360
that thing would slide
right off his shoulder.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:44.220
I would've personally
liked to have seen in this,

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:47.070
like when Houdini did the
100-foot-rope tie challenge,

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:48.630
he'd be on a chair just like this,

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:50.250
but the rope would be
wrapped around his neck

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:51.930
and under his arms and through his legs.

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:54.296
Rope escapes, as a rule,

00:02:54.296 --> 00:02:57.279
require a lot more pure strength

00:02:57.279 --> 00:02:59.520
than, say, doing a handcuff escape, right?

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:02.370
Because a handcuff escape is
really not using strength.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:04.200
It's using dexterity.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:05.972
You've got to manipulate your body

00:03:05.972 --> 00:03:08.340
to get out of a rope
or something like that.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:09.690
Henry: What am I looking for?

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:11.520
Indiana: My lucky charm.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.800
Try and burn through the ropes.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.354
I don't know how you're getting your hand

00:03:15.354 --> 00:03:17.580
from here up to that rope.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:20.125
I don't know how fast

00:03:20.125 --> 00:03:21.658
that fire's going to
burn through that rope.

00:03:21.658 --> 00:03:23.580
I've done upside-down straitjacket escapes

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:25.320
where I've had the rope on fire,

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:28.410
and, you know, I'm 220
pounds, hanging upside down.

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:30.210
There's a lot of weight on that rope.

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:32.580
It takes a while for that
rope to burn through.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.380
It depends what kind
of rope it is as well.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:37.937
If you go to, like, a
hardware store and buy rope,

00:03:37.937 --> 00:03:41.710
a lot of it is a plastic.
The fibers are plastic.

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:45.300
If this was a plastic
rope, then it might work.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:48.030
It might go through, but
obviously a hemp rope

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:49.413
or a cotton rope or something,

00:03:49.413 --> 00:03:51.930
it's going to take,
especially a rope that thick,

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:54.480
I don't think you're going to
burn through that very easily.

00:03:55.479 --> 00:03:57.376
I think I can get these ropes off.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:03:59.393
Come on.

00:03:59.393 --> 00:04:01.020
That was the only part
of this escape to me

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:02.197
that kind of looked legitimate,

00:04:02.197 --> 00:04:04.020
is his hands were tied with a rope.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:07.050
If they were tied legitimately around,

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:08.298
cinched up to my hands,

00:04:08.298 --> 00:04:09.990
it would take you some time to get out.

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:11.790
It's not physically impossible.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:13.890
You've got to kind of deform
your hand a little bit,

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:16.560
crush it down, get it
closer together, you know?

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.240
You're going to get
some rope burns from it.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:21.480
If somebody's going to tie
your wrists with a rope,

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:24.210
a good tip is -- how
can I demonstrate this?

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:25.590
Imagine this is a rope.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:28.830
Imagine if that's put around
my wrists like this, OK?

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:32.250
A good tip is, if somebody's
coming in to tie my wrist,

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:34.100
is to just bring the pinky in like this

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:36.510
and grab an extra bit.

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:37.860
Because if this now goes around

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:41.190
and is locked on top of my
hands, if I just let that go,

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:43.002
I've got enough slack to get my hand out.

00:04:43.002 --> 00:04:44.460
Look at the rope.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:46.200
The rope's, like, this thick.

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:49.860
It is difficult to tie a thicker rope

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:52.050
as tight as it is a thin rope.

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:54.327
The chances of getting
out of that kind of rope

00:04:54.327 --> 00:04:56.035
are going to be a lot better

00:04:56.035 --> 00:04:57.493
than getting out of a smaller rope.

00:04:57.493 --> 00:04:59.910
Because it's around the
thickest part of his leg, here.

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:02.070
So if it's around the
thickest part of your leg,

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:03.780
it can go over the thinnest part.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:06.240
Not like if a rope's tied
here, around the thinnest part,

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.310
it can't go over the thickest
part of your hand, right?

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:11.010
I actually might have to give it a 1,

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:12.720
because I really think

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:14.820
they could have done a lot more homework

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:16.670
to make it look a lot more realistic.

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:23.485
If ever you're in a situation like this,

00:05:23.485 --> 00:05:25.563
right, heaven forbid,

00:05:25.563 --> 00:05:29.130
you've got to assess the
situation around you first.

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:31.138
So, for example, here,

00:05:31.138 --> 00:05:34.546
the immediate thought is to
try and get that zip tie off,

00:05:34.546 --> 00:05:36.600
but maybe the radiator's loose

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:38.473
and maybe the paper that's up there

00:05:38.473 --> 00:05:40.070
is held up by drawing pins

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:42.540
and he could pull one out
and use that to cut the zip.

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:45.048
I think what I'd be looking
for in this situation is maybe

00:05:45.048 --> 00:05:49.177
something that I could put
through that I could turn.

00:05:49.177 --> 00:05:50.880
That's going to put a lot
of pressure on the wrist,

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:53.747
but at some point, something has to give.

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:00.420
It is possible, using a
technique, to get zip ties off.

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:01.920
This one is slightly different

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:04.620
because his hands aren't
zip-tied together.

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:06.360
He's zip-tied to the radiator.

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:08.157
These aren't what law
enforcement would use.

00:06:08.157 --> 00:06:10.242
It's pretty small. It sounds weird,

00:06:10.242 --> 00:06:12.125
but the zip tie's got to
be as tight as it can.

00:06:12.125 --> 00:06:16.080
If you look at my chest and
my body and look at my arms,

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:19.740
they're wider than this zip tie, right?

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:23.081
So if I were to bring these hands down

00:06:23.081 --> 00:06:25.747
against my chest, here,

00:06:25.747 --> 00:06:30.330
with force and with conviction,
and as I come to here,

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:32.730
I kind of push my shoulder
blades at the back,

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:34.023
I push them together.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:36.750
It'll just snap the zip tie.

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:39.180
I personally would never get
out of a zip tie like that,

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:42.150
but having said that, he is
attached to the radiator,

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:44.790
so I don't know that force would work.

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:46.748
I'm maybe going to give it a 6.

00:06:57.216 --> 00:06:58.710
Picking a lock is not easy.

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:00.150
People ask me how I learned to pick locks.

00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:01.380
This is a practice lock.

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:05.100
You can see it's clear, so
you can see the pins inside.

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:06.570
Because essentially, when
a key goes in a lock,

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:09.445
it just moves, all those
little indentations in the key

00:07:09.445 --> 00:07:12.420
move the pins into the
right, correct format,

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:14.160
and then it will open the lock, right?

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:17.160
So if I wanted to pick this lock,

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:19.980
so you'd need a pick and
you'd need a pry bar,

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:23.850
because a pry bar puts pressure
on the lock as you pick it,

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:27.270
so that when you get the pins
in the right place, it opens.

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:28.740
That is exactly what she's doing there.

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:32.520
Now, it looks like she's using a hairpin

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:34.261
or maybe two hairpins.

00:07:34.261 --> 00:07:37.468
Whether or not the hairpin
would be strong enough

00:07:37.468 --> 00:07:39.750
as a pry bar to put pressure on the lock,

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:41.760
essentially the technique
she's using there

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:45.853
to open that lock is legitimate.

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:50.340
She's pulling it along the pins to try

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:51.780
and rake them into the right position.

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:53.790
And what happens is, by
putting that pressure on,

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:56.370
as soon as a pin goes into
the right position, it holds,

00:07:56.370 --> 00:07:58.500
and then you go in, you
rake, another one holds,

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:01.980
and that's always moving
until, click, it goes.

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:04.404
I would actually give this a
rating of, like, 8 out of 10.

00:08:04.404 --> 00:08:06.337
The only reason I wouldn't
give it a 10 is because

00:08:06.337 --> 00:08:10.500
I don't know if those
tools would do that job,

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:12.510
but she's certainly using
them in the right way,

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:13.994
and the technique's right.

00:08:13.994 --> 00:08:16.369
When that timer hits 0,

00:08:16.369 --> 00:08:19.401
a tank full of flesh-eating
piranhas will fall from ...

00:08:19.401 --> 00:08:22.050
They're putting leg
cuffs on, chains, locks.

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:24.120
I mean, this is all pretty standard fare.

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:25.500
Those cuffs look strong.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:29.071
The chains are good,
strong chain, padlocks.

00:08:29.071 --> 00:08:30.903
[buzzer rings]

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:33.510
First of all, if we stop there,

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:35.070
you notice she was standing on a platform,

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:37.290
and then the platform
released and she drops in.

00:08:37.290 --> 00:08:40.275
I would never do it that
way because I'd be worried

00:08:40.275 --> 00:08:42.990
about cracking my head
or something like that.

00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:45.420
So, I use a tank that's 10 feet high,

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:48.150
about that height, but very narrow.

00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:50.520
And so what I do is climb
a ladder all chained up

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:52.860
and then kind of inch myself to the edge,

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:55.410
and get down into the water on my elbows

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:57.558
so that when I do go under,

00:08:57.558 --> 00:09:00.017
I've not got a chance of hitting my head.

00:09:04.650 --> 00:09:05.640
No, OK, stop.

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:07.758
She's using nothing to pick those.

00:09:07.758 --> 00:09:10.166
There's nothing. I can't see anything.

00:09:10.166 --> 00:09:11.550
She's not using a lockpick.

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:13.642
She's just pulling at them,
and then they come off.

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:19.410
Houdini, when he used to
do the underwater escape,

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:22.050
they would stand by with a
sledgehammer to break the glass.

00:09:22.050 --> 00:09:24.960
When I was in New York getting
ready to perform on Broadway

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:27.900
and I had an accident and I
blacked out and I drowned,

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:29.970
it was quicker for them
to shatter the tank

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:32.070
with a sledgehammer than
it was to use the keys.

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:33.900
What he's using there is, like, a bar.

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:35.490
I don't think that's going to do the job,

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:37.366
because that glass is
going to be really thick.

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:45.000
She does hold her breath there
for about a minute, I think.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:46.140
That's certainly not out of the way.

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:48.729
The average person can hold their breath,

00:09:48.729 --> 00:09:52.740
you know, 30 seconds to a
minute and a half, 90 seconds.

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:55.320
If I go in a swimming
pool and I just relax

00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:57.060
and don't move, I can hold my breath

00:09:57.060 --> 00:09:58.860
for about four and a half, five minutes,

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:02.640
because my heart rate lowers
and it's not burning oxygen

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:04.020
because I'm completely relaxed.

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:05.333
But if I go in a tank of water

00:10:05.333 --> 00:10:08.010
and chain and lock myself
up in front of 1,000 people

00:10:08.010 --> 00:10:09.510
and they lock the lid,

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:12.660
you better believe my
heart rate goes so high

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:17.130
and burns my oxygen in, like, 20 seconds.

00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:19.170
The actual record for holding breath,

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:21.420
I believe, is about 24 minutes.

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:24.008
An actual apnea hold,
without purging oxygen,

00:10:24.008 --> 00:10:26.324
just [inhales] and holding,

00:10:26.324 --> 00:10:29.657
for a man is about 11 minutes.

00:10:29.657 --> 00:10:31.503
For a lady, it's about eight minutes.

00:10:33.690 --> 00:10:36.210
Look how much water is coming
out of that little fish tank.

00:10:36.210 --> 00:10:37.620
That's way too much water.

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:39.390
There's a dude up top
there with a big hose

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:41.040
and a pump or something.

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.140
So, if you're in a venue
doing an underwater escape,

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:44.910
one of the things they
will always say to you is,

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:46.290
"Is the water going to go over the top?

00:10:46.290 --> 00:10:47.580
Because it's going to wreck the stage.

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:49.830
It's going to wreck the
cameras, the cables."

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:52.380
You might have a bit of an
overflow for dramatic effect,

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:56.580
but you would never, ever
have that much water.

00:10:56.580 --> 00:10:58.862
[crowd screaming]

00:10:58.862 --> 00:11:01.931
Whoever thought of this is a sick sadist!

00:11:01.931 --> 00:11:03.750
[crowd cheers]

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:05.910
This bit's cool because
this is the classic,

00:11:05.910 --> 00:11:07.293
they appear at the back of the audience

00:11:07.293 --> 00:11:08.673
behind the judges, right?

00:11:08.673 --> 00:11:10.473
Is this legitimate? Absolutely.

00:11:10.473 --> 00:11:12.840
Could it be done the way
they've done it in the show?

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:13.673
I don't think so.

00:11:13.673 --> 00:11:16.573
Maybe, like, a 4, I guess,

00:11:16.573 --> 00:11:20.580
on a premise of a performance.

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:22.497
Take me halfway around.

00:11:27.690 --> 00:11:30.303
The coffin looks like a magic prop, right?

00:11:30.303 --> 00:11:33.398
Nobody's going to buy that
there's absolutely nothing --

00:11:33.398 --> 00:11:35.070
"There's nothing wrong with this coffin!

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:37.230
It doesn't have any
trapdoors of any kind."

00:11:37.230 --> 00:11:39.270
I mean, it looks like a magic prop,

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:41.700
which you would expect from this show.

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:44.550
I will become my dead father's body

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:46.650
as I am lowered into his grave.

00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:49.530
Buried alive is a classic plot in escapes.

00:11:49.530 --> 00:11:50.820
You've got to get out of the coffin,

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:52.530
however means that happens.

00:11:52.530 --> 00:11:53.905
But then you've still got to get through

00:11:53.905 --> 00:11:55.713
5 or 6 feet of dirt,

00:11:55.713 --> 00:11:58.005
and you've got to escape before
you get out of the coffin.

00:11:58.005 --> 00:12:02.190
There was a magician,
Joe Burrus, from the US,

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:06.630
who did a buried-alive escape and got out.

00:12:06.630 --> 00:12:08.490
But then a few years later on Halloween,

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:10.590
he did another version of it and died.

00:12:10.590 --> 00:12:13.650
Because what he did was he'd
put 5 feet of dirt on top,

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:16.770
but then he put a foot
or 2 feet of concrete,

00:12:16.770 --> 00:12:18.030
poured concrete.

00:12:18.030 --> 00:12:19.200
Well, David Blaine's was different.

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:23.305
He was in a clear box with clear glass,

00:12:23.305 --> 00:12:25.170
I think, on top, so people could see him.

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:27.510
So there wasn't any chance
of something collapsing.

00:12:27.510 --> 00:12:30.120
Keep it together, Buster,
and spin this back around.

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:33.016
Oh, my God, the card! The card!

00:12:34.563 --> 00:12:37.530
This is why we have this rap.

00:12:37.530 --> 00:12:39.690
This is what all this beard and wax

00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:42.366
and all these tattoos is trying to dispel,

00:12:42.366 --> 00:12:46.290
is that myth that we're all
kids-party magicians and hacks.

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:47.970
I mean, would you hire
me for your kid's party?

00:12:47.970 --> 00:12:48.840
I don't think so.

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:52.950
Buster is the perfect assistant for this,

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:55.020
although I don't think the character means

00:12:55.020 --> 00:12:55.920
for it to go wrong.

00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:58.260
You very rarely see my assistants.

00:12:58.260 --> 00:13:00.300
My assistants are in the wings,

00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:02.070
making sure that I don't die.

00:13:02.070 --> 00:13:03.450
So, people that know CPR.

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:05.670
People that have got extra
keys if something goes wrong,

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:08.730
sledgehammers, medics, things like that.

00:13:08.730 --> 00:13:09.930
And we've had to use them.

00:13:09.930 --> 00:13:12.990
I mean, for escape, you'd
have to give it a 1.

00:13:12.990 --> 00:13:14.100
But for comedy value,

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:17.073
you'd have to give it
a 15 or a 20 out of 10.

00:13:19.350 --> 00:13:22.020
Can you confirm that the straps are tight?

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:24.175
The way he's got the
straitjacket on right now

00:13:24.175 --> 00:13:26.025
is not a way that any escape artist

00:13:26.025 --> 00:13:27.575
would ever wear a straitjacket.

00:13:27.575 --> 00:13:29.160
The arms have to be crossed

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:32.040
with each wrist under the elbow, right?

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:34.380
So that when it's
tightened up on the back,

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:35.788
there is no way you can physically

00:13:35.788 --> 00:13:37.163
get the arm over the elbow.

00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:44.491
Do you know what? I'm
not saying escape artists

00:13:44.491 --> 00:13:47.243
don't maybe hide keys. I know Houdini,

00:13:47.243 --> 00:13:50.460
people said he hid keys and things around.

00:13:50.460 --> 00:13:53.280
I would never put a key in my mouth,

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:55.650
mainly because when I'm physically moving,

00:13:55.650 --> 00:13:57.750
it would be very easy for
me to swallow that key

00:13:57.750 --> 00:14:00.375
and choke on the thing,
whether it's an extra key

00:14:00.375 --> 00:14:04.110
or, if you're using a
lockpick, an extra lockpick.

00:14:04.110 --> 00:14:05.340
You've got to have an out.

00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:06.600
When I was on "Britain's Got Talent,"

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:08.220
I dropped a lockpick while I was trying

00:14:08.220 --> 00:14:10.770
to get out of a head tank full of water,

00:14:10.770 --> 00:14:12.920
and I had to use the
bar to break the lock.

00:14:14.280 --> 00:14:17.700
Michael: I cannot tell
you how I plan to escape,

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:20.040
other than by using magic.

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:21.870
You know, there's a lot of physicalness

00:14:21.870 --> 00:14:23.460
to getting out of a straitjacket.

00:14:23.460 --> 00:14:24.990
You've got to thrash around.

00:14:24.990 --> 00:14:27.270
You know, you get cuts, you get bruises.

00:14:27.270 --> 00:14:28.740
It hurts.

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:31.620
I've burned myself. I've
bruised myself. I've cut myself.

00:14:31.620 --> 00:14:35.077
That writhing around on
the ground is pretty much,

00:14:35.077 --> 00:14:36.750
you know, how we do it.

00:14:36.750 --> 00:14:38.550
Straitjacket escape is difficult.

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:40.170
Houdini used to do the straitjacket escape

00:14:40.170 --> 00:14:41.970
in every city that he ever did a show at.

00:14:41.970 --> 00:14:43.838
There are tips and tricks that we use

00:14:43.838 --> 00:14:45.420
to try and make that job a little easier,

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:48.780
but people have dislocated
limbs and broken limbs

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:50.880
and all kinds of things trying to do it.

00:14:50.880 --> 00:14:52.985
So, is it true that if you can't get out,

00:14:52.985 --> 00:14:54.690
you don't want anyone to help you?

00:14:54.690 --> 00:14:55.770
I will get out.

00:14:55.770 --> 00:14:57.330
Oh, yes, I will.

00:14:57.330 --> 00:14:59.922
Not accepting help if you get stuck

00:14:59.922 --> 00:15:03.000
is not the way a professional
escape artist does things.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:05.340
I always have a team on standby,

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:06.600
and I've had to use them more than once.

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:07.980
I've drowned twice.

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:10.290
I'm giving him a 2 because
he's in the straitjacket

00:15:10.290 --> 00:15:12.240
and he's put chains on,
but I mean, you know,

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:14.700
he's not even wearing
the straitjacket right.

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:16.900
Maybe a magic word or two,

00:15:19.291 --> 00:15:20.850
and then I'll be gone.

00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:22.710
One of the first tricks I ever got shown

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:24.690
when I was a kid was something like that,

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:26.850
where he's doing the ball thing, right?

00:15:26.850 --> 00:15:31.567
So he's going one, two, and
like this, and it's gone.

00:15:31.567 --> 00:15:33.090
So now look how his hand is.

00:15:33.090 --> 00:15:35.700
He's holding the ball, but
it's classic misdirection,

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:40.350
because if I just go like
this and don't look up at it,

00:15:40.350 --> 00:15:42.870
when I put it in this hand,
you see me put it there.

00:15:42.870 --> 00:15:45.840
But if my eyes are following the object,

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:47.490
that means your eyes are
following the object.

00:15:47.490 --> 00:15:50.580
Distraction for something
like this, 100% important.

00:15:50.580 --> 00:15:51.413
Gone.

00:15:56.490 --> 00:15:59.350
That quick, without
the guard feeling that,

00:16:00.330 --> 00:16:02.220
I would say is nigh on impossible.

00:16:02.220 --> 00:16:05.610
Maybe if you set off a
firecracker, you know, or a bomb,

00:16:05.610 --> 00:16:07.890
it might have worked,
but just dropping a ball,

00:16:07.890 --> 00:16:11.100
and me going for it,
that switch, that quick,

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:12.270
pretty much impossible,

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:14.460
considering that these are
real chains, real padlocks.

00:16:14.460 --> 00:16:17.160
But if you look at the cuff
on his right wrist there,

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:19.410
there's actually no padlock in this.

00:16:19.410 --> 00:16:22.650
It's essentially a key
that goes in, turns,

00:16:22.650 --> 00:16:25.680
and it's all built into the actual cuff.

00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:27.840
So these are an old-style cuff.

00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:31.170
So the leg irons here are
all built into the leg iron.

00:16:31.170 --> 00:16:33.330
So he's got to unlock the iron

00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:34.560
and then put it around the other guy.

00:16:34.560 --> 00:16:36.090
When he puts it around the other guy,

00:16:36.090 --> 00:16:38.490
it will instantly lock when
he squeezes it together.

00:16:38.490 --> 00:16:40.110
So actually it wouldn't take as much time

00:16:40.110 --> 00:16:41.280
as I initially thought.

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:43.530
I still don't think he's got enough time.

00:16:43.530 --> 00:16:45.210
I'm going to say maybe a 5.

00:16:45.210 --> 00:16:47.580
As long as he picked
it here and opened it,

00:16:47.580 --> 00:16:49.872
he could snap it around the other person.

00:16:53.790 --> 00:16:55.920
Now, I've done a lot of escapes

00:16:55.920 --> 00:16:57.960
where I'm hanging upside down.

00:16:57.960 --> 00:16:59.520
I've never done escapes the right way up.

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:02.280
It looks like she's
maybe tied by the wrists

00:17:02.280 --> 00:17:05.550
with rope or handcuffs or
something that's hooked up.

00:17:05.550 --> 00:17:08.348
I couldn't lift up a bag of sugar,

00:17:08.348 --> 00:17:12.381
never mind a 6-foot-2,
bald-headed, muscly guy.

00:17:19.439 --> 00:17:22.080
[laughing]

00:17:22.080 --> 00:17:26.220
OK, I mean, listen, have you ever tried

00:17:26.220 --> 00:17:29.520
to get a key in your front-door
lock when you come home

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:31.140
and it's nigh on impossible?

00:17:31.140 --> 00:17:33.672
That kind of position,

00:17:33.672 --> 00:17:37.380
to then even lift your
legs is really hard,

00:17:37.380 --> 00:17:39.210
unless you have really good core muscles.

00:17:39.210 --> 00:17:40.980
So, I have to use my hands against my legs

00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:42.840
to kind of walk myself up there.

00:17:42.840 --> 00:17:46.710
I'd maybe give this a 5, I'm going to say.

00:17:46.710 --> 00:17:50.712
My favorite escape in a movie or TV

00:17:50.712 --> 00:17:53.910
would have to be something
called the water-cube trap,

00:17:53.910 --> 00:17:57.210
which was actually in the
"Saw" series of horror movies.

00:17:57.210 --> 00:17:58.590
And essentially it was a little cube,

00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:01.320
and when the guy woke up,
his head was in this cube.

00:18:01.320 --> 00:18:02.913
There was two pipes coming in

00:18:02.913 --> 00:18:05.790
attached to water gallon cylinders,

00:18:05.790 --> 00:18:07.033
and then the water started coming in

00:18:07.033 --> 00:18:08.735
and filling up slowly, right?

00:18:08.735 --> 00:18:11.610
And I had a very, very
similar-looking device,

00:18:11.610 --> 00:18:13.770
which was a box attached
to a metal straitjacket

00:18:13.770 --> 00:18:15.210
hanging from chains.

00:18:15.210 --> 00:18:18.210
And I was all padlocked and chained in,

00:18:18.210 --> 00:18:20.790
and then they filled it with
water and locked the box.

00:18:20.790 --> 00:18:22.475
Hey, thanks so much for watching, guys.

00:18:22.475 --> 00:18:24.241
I'm Matt Johnson, and if you want to watch

00:18:24.241 --> 00:18:25.566
other videos like this,

00:18:25.566 --> 00:18:28.313
make sure you go ahead
and click right here.

